\title{Thermal Stability and Structural Integrity of Multi-Walled Carbon Nanotubes Under High-Temperature Catalytic Conditions}

\author{Sarah Chen}
\email{schen@berkeley.edu}
\affiliation{Department of Materials Science and Engineering, University of California, Berkeley, California 94720, USA}

\author{Michael Rodriguez}
\affiliation{Department of Materials Science and Engineering, University of California, Berkeley, California 94720, USA}

\author{James Park}
\affiliation{Department of Materials Science and Engineering, University of California, Berkeley, California 94720, USA}

\date{\today}

\begin{abstract}
This study investigates the thermal stability and structural evolution of multi-walled carbon nanotubes (MWCNTs) under high-temperature conditions in the presence of iron-molybdenum (Fe-Mo) bimetallic catalysts. Through systematic variation of temperature (\SIrange{800}{1200}{\celsius}), pressure (\SIrange{1}{5}{\atm}), and catalyst concentration (\SIrange{1.0}{4.0}{\wt\percent}), we identified optimal processing conditions that maximize structural retention while minimizing defect formation. Our results demonstrate that MWCNTs exhibit exceptional stability at \SI{950}{\celsius} with \SI{2.5}{\wt\percent} catalyst loading, achieving structural integrity scores of $0.96$ and Raman D/G ratios of $0.82$. However, a critical degradation threshold exists at \SI{1050}{\celsius}, above which catalytic etching mechanisms dominate, resulting in significant wall collapse and diameter reduction. Thermogravimetric analysis reveals weight loss kinetics consistent with defect-mediated oxidation processes. These findings establish critical process windows for high-temperature applications of carbon nanotubes in catalytic environments.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{carbon nanotubes, thermal stability, catalytic etching, defect engineering, high-temperature materials}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Carbon nanotubes (CNTs) have emerged as promising candidates for high-temperature applications due to their exceptional thermal conductivity and mechanical strength.\cite{dresselhaus2010} However, their structural integrity under catalytic conditions at elevated temperatures remains poorly characterized, particularly for multi-walled configurations where inter-wall interactions complicate degradation mechanisms.\cite{popov2004} Previous studies have focused primarily on single-walled nanotubes or oxidative environments, leaving a critical knowledge gap regarding catalytic stability in inert atmospheres.

The presence of metallic catalysts, essential for CNT synthesis and functionalization, introduces additional complexity. Transition metal nanoparticles can catalyze carbon gasification reactions at temperatures significantly lower than thermal oxidation thresholds,\cite{chen2023} potentially compromising nanotube structure during processing or operation. Understanding the interplay between thermal effects and catalytic etching is essential for designing robust CNT-based materials for aerospace, energy, and electronic applications.

\section{Experimental Methods}

Multi-walled carbon nanotubes (diameter \SI{15\pm2}{\nm}, length \SIrange{5}{20}{\micro\meter}, purity $>95\%$) were obtained from commercial sources and dispersed in ethanol with Fe-Mo catalyst nanoparticles (average diameter \SI{5}{\nm}). Samples were treated in a tube furnace under argon atmosphere with controlled temperature, pressure, and duration parameters. Post-treatment characterization included Raman spectroscopy (\SI{514}{\nm} excitation), transmission electron microscopy (TEM), and thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) in air from \SIrange{25}{800}{\celsius} at \SI{10}{\celsius\per\minute}.

