\section{Results and Discussion}

\subsection{Temperature Dependence and Critical Thresholds}

Our systematic temperature study reveals a non-linear relationship between processing temperature and structural degradation. At \SI{800}{\celsius} (Experiment CNT-2024-001), nanotubes exhibited minimal degradation with structural integrity scores of $0.94$ and low defect densities (\SI{1.2e11}{\per\centi\meter\squared}). Increasing the temperature to \SI{950}{\celsius} (CNT-2024-002) actually improved structural metrics, with the D/G ratio decreasing to $0.82$ and integrity scores reaching $0.96$, suggesting mild annealing effects that heal synthesis-induced defects.

However, exceeding \SI{1050}{\celsius} (CNT-2024-008) triggered accelerated degradation, with integrity scores dropping to $0.81$ and defect densities increasing to \SI{2.1e12}{\per\centi\meter\squared}. At \SI{1200}{\celsius} (CNT-2024-005), catastrophic failure occurred, with \SI{45.2}{\percent} weight loss and severe fragmentation observed via TEM. This threshold behavior suggests a transition from thermally activated defect healing to catalytic etching dominance.

The degradation kinetics follow an Arrhenius-type relationship below the critical threshold:
\begin{equation}
k = A \exp\left(-\frac{E_a}{RT}\right)
\end{equation}
where $E_a$ represents the activation energy for defect migration and healing (\SI{\sim1.2}{\eV} for $T < \SI{1000}{\celsius}$), transitioning to etching activation energies (\SI{\sim2.8}{\eV}) above \SI{1050}{\celsius}.

\subsection{Catalyst Concentration Optimization}

Catalyst loading significantly influenced stability outcomes. At \SI{950}{\celsius}, reducing catalyst concentration to \SI{1.0}{\wt\percent} (CNT-2024-001) resulted in incomplete defect healing, while increasing to \SI{4.0}{\wt\percent} (CNT-2024-006) caused excessive etching and \SI{14.8}{\percent} weight loss. The optimal concentration of \SI{2.5}{\wt\percent} (CNT-2024-002) balanced defect remediation against material removal, preserving $96\%$ of structural integrity over 4-hour treatments.

\subsection{Pressure Effects and Kinetics}

Elevated pressure environments (\SIrange{3}{5}{\atm}) suppressed degradation kinetics. At \SI{950}{\celsius} with \SI{2.5}{\wt\percent} catalyst, increasing pressure from \SI{1}{\atm} to \SI{5}{\atm} (CNT-2024-009) improved structural integrity from $0.96$ to $0.97$ and reduced weight loss from \SI{1.8}{\percent} to \SI{1.7}{\percent}. This modest but reproducible effect suggests pressure-dependent inhibition of carbon gasification reactions at catalyst-nanotube interfaces.

\subsection{Thermogravimetric Analysis}

TGA profiles revealed distinct oxidation behaviors correlating with treatment conditions. Samples processed at optimal conditions (\SI{950}{\celsius}, \SI{2.5}{\wt\percent}) showed single-step oxidation with onset temperatures of \SI{580}{\celsius}, indicating uniform wall structures. In contrast, high-temperature degraded samples (\SI{1100}{\celsius}, \SI{1200}{\celsius}) exhibited multi-step degradation with early onset at \SI{450}{\celsius}, consistent with defect-mediated oxidation pathways and wall thinning.

The correlation between Raman D/G ratios and structural integrity scores ($R^2 = 0.94$) validates Raman spectroscopy as a rapid, non-destructive screening tool for thermal damage assessment in carbon nanotube materials.

\section{Conclusions}

This study establishes definitive process windows for high-temperature processing of MWCNTs in catalytic environments. The optimal conditions---\SI{950}{\celsius}, \SI{2.5}{\wt\percent} Fe-Mo catalyst, 4-hour duration---maximize structural retention while minimizing defect formation. The identification of a critical degradation threshold at \SI{1050}{\celsius} provides crucial safety margins for industrial applications. 

The observed stability window represents a balance between thermal energy sufficient for atomic rearrangement and defect healing, yet insufficient to activate catalytic carbon gasification. The Fe-Mo catalyst system facilitates sp$^2$ network reconstruction at moderate temperatures while promoting destructive etching above the \SI{1050}{\celsius} threshold.

Future work should investigate the atomic-scale mechanisms of the \SI{950}{\celsius} annealing effect and explore catalyst encapsulation strategies to extend the operational temperature range beyond current limits. The pressure dependence observed suggests that high-pressure processing environments may offer additional stabilization for critical applications.

